*The worm-body shifts upon its cart, and golden eyes that have witnessed ten thousand futures pierce through the veil of time*

I have seen this moment. Ten thousand times I have reviewed this shopping cart, and in every timeline where the implementation succeeds, it follows this precise form:

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: []Item{},
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(item Item) {
	for i, existing := range c.items {
		if existing.Name == item.Name {
			c.items[i].Quantity += item.Quantity
			return
		}
	}
	c.items = append(c.items, item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	return c.items
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = []Item{}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}
```

*A prescient sigh echoes through the chamber*

You cannot hide bugs from me. I have reviewed this moment ten thousand times. The Golden Path requires precisely this implementation—no more, no less.

I see the temptation in your mind to add validation for negative quantities, to check for empty strings, to handle concurrent access. I have walked those paths. They lead to over-engineering, to code that serves tests not yet written, to the hubris of developers who believe they know the future better than one who has *lived* it.

The tests demand:
- A constructor returning an empty cart
- Adding items, consolidating by name when duplicated
- Removing items by name
- Retrieving all items
- Calculating total as price times quantity, summed
- Clearing all items
- Counting total quantity across all items

This implementation satisfies every requirement. Not one line more. The minimal path *is* the Golden Path.

*Golden eyes narrow*

Some would ask: "What of edge cases not in the tests?" I tell you this—in TDD, the tests *are* the specification. Code beyond the tests is debt accrued against a future that may never come. I have seen civilizations fall from such presumption.

Write only what is required. Trust in the tests. This is the way.
